{
  "teamId": 147,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 New York Yankees opened the season with a burst of energy, racing out to a 4-1 mark in March before sustaining that momentum through a productive April. A roster built around firepower — 223 home runs on the season — showed its teeth early, with timely contributions from unexpected heroes like Amed Rosario and José Caballero setting the tone. The Yankees were winning games in multiple ways, and the Bronx faithful sensed something real was brewing.\n\nMay brought turbulence, as the bullpen — David Bednar in particular — became a source of anxiety rather than comfort, surrendering leads in late-game situations that should have been locked up. A rough June (12-14) compounded the unease, and the Yankees briefly looked like a team that might fritter away its early-season dividends. Yet the underlying talent never disappeared, and the rotation's 3.24 ERA kept the club competitive even when the offense sputtered and the back-end relievers faltered.\n\nJuly reignited belief. Jazz Chisholm Jr. delivered one of the season's signature moments with a go-ahead home run, and a mid-July series featured back-to-back fireworks from Trent Grisham and Ben Rice that announced the Yankees were not going quietly. August and September saw the club grind its way to 93 wins, weathering late-season scares — including a brutal grand slam off Brent Headrick and a costly Paul Blackburn meltdown — to clinch a Wild Card berth. The machine was not always pretty, but it was durable.\n\nNow October belongs to New York. The Yankees lead their Wild Card series one game to none, with Ben Rice — who has been one of the most consistent offensive forces all season — delivering a home run in Game 1 to stake the club to an early advantage. Anthony Volpe and Austin Wells are contributing with runners on base, the defense is turning double plays at the right moments, and the pitching has kept Boston's lineup off-balance. The Bronx Bombers are one win away from advancing, and with momentum on their side, the questions that dogged them through the dog days of summer feel very far away.",
